{"cuisine/komi-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/komi-cuisine","n":"Komi cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Russia","intro":[["Komi cuisine consists of the cuisine of the Komi Republic and the Komi peoples, and is characterized by the rich use of local foods."],["Significant differences separate Komis' dining preferences in the northern and southern regions of their homeland. In the northern reindeer herding and hunting areas, meat is eaten daily, but it is not in the more agricultural south. In the south, fish is commonly eaten; hogs and poultry are eaten less often."],["Komi women bake fish pie on festive family occasions. The Fish Pie Festival (Komi: Черинянь гаж) is held annually on the last Sunday of June in the village of Byzovaya, Pechora Raion."],["Traditional Komi beverages include kvas and sur, a type of ale."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"komi-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Komi_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Sur (Komi beverage)","dish/sur-komi-beverage"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"ingredient/scrambled-eggs":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/scrambled-eggs","n":"Scrambled eggs","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Scrambled eggs is a culinary dish made from eggs (usually chicken eggs), where the whites and yolks have been stirred, whipped, or beaten together (typically with the addition of other ingredients), then heated so that the proteins denature and coagulate, and they form into \"curds\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-scrambled-eggs"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scrambled_eggs"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Akuri","dish/akuri"]]],["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",[["Akuri","dish/akuri"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Mentha","ingredient/mentha"],["Scrambled egg","ingredient/scrambled-egg"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/20/Scrambed_eggs.jpg/500px-Scrambed_eggs.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Takeaway","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scrambed_eggs.jpg"}},"ingredient/bamboo-shoot":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/bamboo-shoot","n":"Bamboo shoot","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Bamboo shoots or bamboo sprouts are the edible shoots (new bamboo culms that come out of the ground) of many bamboo species including Bambusa vulgaris and Phyllostachys edulis. They are used as vegetables in numerous Asian dishes and broths. They are sold in various processed shapes and are available in fresh, dried, and canned versions."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-bamboo-shoot"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamboo_shoot"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Lumpia semarang","dish/lumpia-semarang"]]],["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine",[["Har gow","dish/har-gow"]]],["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine",[["Bah-uân","dish/bah-uan"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],["Dried shrimp","ingredient/dried-shrimp"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Corn starch","ingredient/corn-starch"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Shiitake","ingredient/shiitake"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Anchovy","ingredient/anchovy"],["Bean curd","ingredient/bean-curd"],["Bumbu (seasoning)","ingredient/bumbu-seasoning"],["Caraway seed","ingredient/caraway-seed"],["Carrots","ingredient/carrots"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/1d/Bamboo_sprout2.JPG/500px-Bamboo_sprout2.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Haragayato","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bamboo_sprout2.JPG"}},"ingredient/illicium-verum":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/illicium-verum","n":"Illicium verum","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Illicium verum (star anise or badian, Chinese star anise, star anise seed, star aniseed and star of anise) is a medium-sized evergreen tree native to South China and northeast Vietnam. Its star-shaped pericarp fruits harvested just before ripening are a spice that closely resembles anise in flavor. Its primary production country is China, followed by Vietnam and other Southeast Asian countries. Star anise oil is highly fragrant, used in cooking, perfumery, soaps, toothpastes, mouthwashes, and skin creams. Until 2012, when it switched to using genetically modified E. coli, Roche Pharmaceuticals used up to 90% of the world's annual star anise crop to produce oseltamivir (Tamiflu) via shikimic acid."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-illicium-verum"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illicium_verum"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Rendang","dish/rendang"]]],["Betawi cuisine","cuisine/betawi-cuisine",[["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"]]],["Padang food","cuisine/padang-food",[["Rendang","dish/rendang"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Biancaea sappan","ingredient/biancaea-sappan"],["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Nigella sativa","ingredient/nigella-sativa"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Piper retrofractum","ingredient/piper-retrofractum"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut cream","ingredient/coconut-cream"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Garcinia atroviridis","ingredient/garcinia-atroviridis"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/7c/Illicium_verum_1zz.jpg/500px-Illicium_verum_1zz.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Photo by David J. Stang","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illicium_verum_1zz.jpg"}},"ingredient/baking-powder":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/baking-powder","n":"Baking powder","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 6 dishes across 10 cuisines","intro":[["Baking powder is a dry chemical leavening agent, a mixture of a carbonate or bicarbonate and a weak acid. The base and acid are prevented from reacting prematurely by the inclusion of a desiccant such as cornstarch. Baking powder is used to increase the volume and lighten the texture of baked goods. It works by releasing carbon dioxide gas into a batter or dough through an acid–base reaction, causing bubbles in the wet mixture to expand and thus leavening the mixture."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-baking-powder"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baking_powder"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":10,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Kue ape","dish/kue-ape"],["Kue leker","dish/kue-leker"]]],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"]]],["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Spotted dick","dish/spotted-dick"]]],["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine",[["Oliebol","dish/oliebol"]]],["Egyptian cuisine","cuisine/egyptian-cuisine",[["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Jordanian cuisine","cuisine/jordanian-cuisine",[["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Lebanese cuisine","cuisine/lebanese-cuisine",[["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine",[["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Palestinian cuisine","cuisine/palestinian-cuisine",[["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Syrian cuisine","cuisine/syrian-cuisine",[["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Pandanus","ingredient/pandanus"],["Banana","ingredient/banana"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Vanilla","ingredient/vanilla"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Ribes","ingredient/ribes"],["Succade","ingredient/succade"],["Sultana (grape)","ingredient/sultana-grape"],["Zest","ingredient/zest"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2d/BakingPowder.jpg/500px-BakingPowder.jpg","w":500,"h":502,"by":"Lou Sander","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BakingPowder.jpg"}},"ingredient/dumpling":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/dumpling","n":"Dumpling","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Dumplings are a broad class of dishes that consist of pieces of cooked dough (made from a variety of starchy sources), often wrapped around a filling. The dough can be based on bread, wheat or other flours, or potatoes, and it may be filled with meat, fish, tofu, cheese, vegetables, or a combination. Dumplings may be prepared using a variety of cooking methods and are found in many world cuisines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-dumpling"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dumpling"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Hot pot","dish/hot-pot"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/40/HK_SYP_%E8%A5%BF%E7%87%9F%E7%9B%A4_Sai_Ying_Pun_Kwan_Yick_Building_%E5%90%8D%E6%98%9F%E6%B5%B7%E9%AE%AE%E9%85%92%E5%AE%B6_Star_Seafood_Restaurant_food_%E5%90%9E%E9%BA%B5_Wonton_dim_sum_October_2020_SS2_01.jpg/500px-HK_SYP_%E8%A5%BF%E7%87%9F%E7%9B%A4_Sai_Ying_Pun_Kwan_Yick_Building_%E5%90%8D%E6%98%9F%E6%B5%B7%E9%AE%AE%E9%85%92%E5%AE%B6_Star_Seafood_Restaurant_food_%E5%90%9E%E9%BA%B5_Wonton_dim_sum_October_2020_SS2_01.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Honmustak Chooica","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HK_SYP_%E8%A5%BF%E7%87%9F%E7%9B%A4_Sai_Ying_Pun_Kwan_Yick_Building_%E5%90%8D%E6%98%9F%E6%B5%B7%E9%AE%AE%E9%85%92%E5%AE%B6_Star_Seafood_Restaurant_food_%E5%90%9E%E9%BA%B5_Wonton_dim_sum_October_2020_SS2_01.jpg"}},"ingredient/rum":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rum","n":"Rum","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Rum is a liquor made by fermenting and then distilling sugarcane molasses or sugarcane juice. The distillate, initially a clear liquid, is often aged in barrels. Rum originated in the Caribbean in the 17th century, where it was likely to have been first created by enslaved people on sugar plantations, but has come to be produced in nearly every major sugar-producing region of the world."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rum"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rum"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Klappertaart","dish/klappertaart"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Coconut water","ingredient/coconut-water"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Margarine","ingredient/margarine"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/2/23/A_glass_of_rum.jpg","w":500,"h":776,"by":"Linus Bohman from Malmö, Sweden","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_glass_of_rum.jpg"}},"ingredient/gelatine-dessert":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/gelatine-dessert","n":"Gelatine dessert","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Gelatine desserts (British English) or gelatin desserts (American English) are desserts made with a sweetened and flavoured processed collagen product (gelatine), which makes the dessert \"set\" from a liquid to a soft elastic solid gel. This kind of dessert was first recorded as \"jelly\" by Hannah Glasse in her 18th-century book The Art of Cookery, appearing in a layer of trifle. Jelly recipes are included in the 19th-century cookbooks of the English food writers Eliza Acton and Isabella Beeton."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-gelatine-dessert"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gelatine_dessert"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Strawberry delight","dish/strawberry-delight"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cracker","ingredient/cracker"],["Cream cheese","ingredient/cream-cheese"],["Gelatin","ingredient/gelatin"],["Graham cracker","ingredient/graham-cracker"],["Strawberry","ingredient/strawberry"],["Whipped cream","ingredient/whipped-cream"],["Whipped topping","ingredient/whipped-topping"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/21/Rainbow-Jello-Cut-2004-Jul-30.jpg/500px-Rainbow-Jello-Cut-2004-Jul-30.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rainbow-Jello-Cut-2004-Jul-30.jpg"}},"dish/teiglach":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/teiglach","n":"Teiglach","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Jewish cuisine","dot":"#a99fb5","intro":[["Teiglach, also spelled taiglach or teglach are small, knotted pastries boiled in a honeyed syrup. They are a traditional Ashkenazi Jewish treat for Rosh Hashanah, Sukkot, Simchat Torah, and Purim."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-teiglach"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teiglach"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Honey","ingredient/honey"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Tori Avey: Jewish Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"toriavey.com","note":"Tori Avey's Jewish recipe index, often paired with notes on each dish's history.","url":"https://toriavey.com/jewish-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"The Nosher (My Jewish Learning)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"myjewishlearning.com","note":"Recipe section of the nonprofit My Jewish Learning, covering Ashkenazi, Sephardi and Mizrahi home cooking and holiday food.","url":"https://www.myjewishlearning.com/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Jewish cuisine","links":[["Appetizing store","dish/appetizing-store"],["Bagel","dish/bagel"],["Brisket (Jewish dish)","dish/brisket-jewish-dish"],["Cebularz","dish/cebularz"],["Challah","dish/challah"],["Forshmak","dish/forshmak"],["Gefilte fish","dish/gefilte-fish"],["Gondi (food)","dish/g